//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// The two-socket host the NUMA experiment actually runs on.
//
// `xeon-e5-2666v3.vx` models the same shape on an older part and cannot be
// measured: that part is only reachable as a virtualized c4.8xlarge, whose two
// NUMA nodes turn out to be cosmetic. See the note in that file. This one is a
// bare-metal instance, so the topology the guest sees is the topology the
// hardware has -- there is no hypervisor between them to synthesize it.
//
// EVERY FIGURE BELOW IS UNVERIFIED AND WRITTEN FROM THE SKU RATHER THAN FROM THE
// MACHINE. That is the opposite of how xeon-e5-2666v3.vx was written, and it is
// a temporary state: the first thing to do on the box is re-read them and
// replace each `spec:` line with what the hardware says. In order of how much
// rests on them:
//
//   numactl -H        node count, per-node size, and the SLIT distances
//   lscpu             socket count, cores per socket, model name
//   lscpu -C          LLC size and how many instances of it there are
//   dmidecode -t 4    the UPI link count, which is the least certain input here
//   /proc/meminfo     MemTotal, against the 192 GiB the instance is sold as
//
// A figure that survives that pass loses its UNVERIFIED marker. One that does
// not is a finding, and the file is wrong rather than the machine surprising.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

// ONE NUMA domain: node0's half of the 192 GiB.
//
// `HBM` and `PEER_HBM` are the fleet's role names, not claims about the
// material -- this is DDR4 and there is no HBM near it, exactly as
// xeon-e5-2666v3.vx explains for its own DDR4. The pair says "a tile lives in
// one domain, and reaching it from the other crosses a priced link", which is
// the same relation node-8gpu.vx declares between two GPUs.
//
// spec: UNVERIFIED. c5.metal is sold as 192 GiB across two sockets, so 96 GiB
// per node. `numactl -H` reports the real per-node size and is what this should
// become; expect it slightly under 96 GiB once firmware and kernel reservation
// are taken out, the same gap the E5 file records.
//
// spec: UNVERIFIED bandwidth, derived rather than quoted. Cascade Lake-SP has 6
// DDR4 channels per socket at up to 2933 MT/s, so
//   6 channels x 2933 MT/s x 8 B = 140.8 GB/s per socket.
// That is one socket's memory controllers' peak and not an achievable copy
// rate. It is per node because a node is one socket.
Memory HBM {
  capacity: 96 GiB, bandwidth: 140 GB/s, managed: explicit, scope: device
}

// node1's half. A space of its own, because that is the whole content of NUMA:
// the bytes are reachable, and reaching them costs something that reaching local
// bytes does not.
//
// spec: as `HBM` above -- the two sockets are the same part.
Memory PEER_HBM {
  capacity: 96 GiB, bandwidth: 140 GB/s, managed: explicit, scope: device
}

// The last-level cache. `L2` names the role (last level on die) rather than the
// level number, which on this part is L3.
//
// spec: UNVERIFIED. Cascade Lake-SP carries 1.375 MiB of L3 per core, and this
// part is 24 cores per socket, so 33 MiB per socket -- but the 8275CL is an AWS
// custom part and is commonly quoted at 35.75 MiB, which is 26 cores' worth and
// suggests two cores are present and disabled. `lscpu -C` settles it, and the
// declared figure should be ONE socket's LLC rather than both, because a tile is
// resident in one socket's cache and the other socket's copy is a coherence
// problem rather than extra capacity.
//
// spec: UNVERIFIED bandwidth. Intel publishes no L3 figure for this part; 400
// GB/s is a placeholder of the right order for a mesh-interconnect LLC on a
// 24-core die and no claim rests on it.
Memory L2 {
  within: Memory::HBM, capacity: 33 MiB, bandwidth: 400 GB/s, managed: cached, scope: device
}

// No `SMEM`, for the reason xeon-e5-2666v3.vx sets out at length: the fleet's
// third space is a software-managed scratchpad, x86 has none, and declaring the
// per-core cache under that name would admit a program that wanted guaranteed
// residency and tell it something false. A program that wants a scratchpad
// cannot have one here and finds out at compile time.

// The host seam is degenerate here in the same way: the device IS the host, so
// `Memory::CPU_DRAM` and `Memory::HBM` name the same DDR4 and a transfer is a
// memcpy. Declared at one node's rate, and the model should over-predict it by
// at least 2x because a memcpy reads and writes and so moves two bytes of
// traffic per byte copied.
//
// THE UPI EDGE IS WHAT THIS FILE EXISTS FOR, and it is the least certain figure
// in it. Cascade Lake carries UPI at 10.4 GT/s, 2 bytes per transfer, so
//   10.4 GT/s x 2 B = 20.8 GB/s per link per direction.
// Platinum parts have three UPI links, and a two-socket system may wire two or
// three of them between the pair. Three gives 62.4 GB/s; two gives 41.6. This
// file assumes three, which is the optimistic end, and a bound that is wrong in
// the optimistic direction is the wrong kind of wrong -- so confirm the link
// count with `dmidecode -t 4` before trusting any prediction derived from it.
//
// Against a node's own 140 GB/s that predicts a remote access costs about 2.3x a
// local one. On two links it would be 3.4x. The measurement settles both the
// ratio and the link count at once: run utils/campaign/run_numa_probe.sh, which
// compares its measured ratio against whatever this file declares.
Topology Device {
  // spec: UNVERIFIED. Intel Cascade Lake-SP, as a compute target rather than a host.
  arch: x86_64,
  memory: Memory::HBM,
  visible: [Memory::HBM, Memory::PEER_HBM, Memory::L2],
  transfer Memory::CPU_DRAM -> Memory::HBM : 140 GB/s,
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::L2,
  transfer Memory::HBM -> Memory::PEER_HBM : 62 GB/s,
  transfer Memory::PEER_HBM -> Memory::HBM : 62 GB/s
}
